            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Testing this on QEMU isn't sufficient and I don't think we can remove this.  We use this for any BMC that has a \"fixed NIC\", rather than an NC-SI NIC, meaning the NIC-type device is hardwired to the BMC.  Bletchley, Minerva-CMM, Ventura all use this style of network connection.\n\nIn those systems, the MAC address comes from an EEPROM.  If you don't have that EEPROM properly modeled in QEMU (which we don't anywhere), you're not running equivalent code to what we run on real hardware.  EEPROM is parsed, loaded by \"entity-manager\" configs, and then the MacAddress field is detected by phosphor-networkd to write an `/etc/systemd/network/00-bmc-eth0.network` file.  That's the path that the MAC gets fed to systemd-networkd.\n\nYou'll see here (https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-networkd/blob/84f58c2d517721f46e362261a7010cee5a8bacac/src/ethernet_interface.cpp#L877) that phosphor-networkd currently always writes `ClientIdentifier=mac`.  Meta wants DUID, so Quanta came up with this hacky script rather than fixing phosphor-networkd so we can properly configure this (or even better tell it not to rewrite the entire config since we already have that `01-bmc-eth.network` file).  Until we get that done, we need this code still otherwise we'll end up with real hardware going back to MAC mode.\n\nNC-SI NICs don't follow this path because the NIC itself keeps the MAC, so there is never an entity-manager \"xyz.openbmc_project....MacAddress\" dbus object that triggers this whole phosphor-networkd flow."
                }
            ],
